<div class="bhd-page__keypoints" aria-label="Endurance hydration principles"><div><strong>Duration matters</strong><span>Longer sessions create more opportunity for fluid, sodium and carbohydrate deficits to accumulate.</span></div><div><strong>Fuel and fluid interact</strong><span>A hydration drink can contribute carbohydrate, but endurance fueling often requires additional food or concentrated carbohydrate sources.</span></div><div><strong>Practice the plan</strong><span>Race-day intake should be tested in training for taste, gut comfort, access and pacing.</span></div></div>

<section class="bhd-page__feature"><div><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Long-duration exercise</p><h2>Endurance is a logistics problem as much as a physiology problem</h2><p>During prolonged exercise, the athlete has to manage several moving targets at once: body heat, sweat loss, thirst, carbohydrate use, gastrointestinal tolerance and access to aid stations or carried supplies.</p><p>That is why a strategy that works for a 40-minute session may be inadequate for a multi-hour event. As duration increases, carbohydrate-electrolyte drinks can become more useful, but they still need to fit into the athlete's complete fueling plan.</p></div></section>

<section class="bhd-page__split"><div class="bhd-page__split-media"><img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/10226372/pexels-photo-10226372.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1500" alt="Runner drinking from a bottle during a marathon" width="1500" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div><div class="bhd-page__split-copy"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Individual losses</p><h2>Use the event to estimate what you need to carry or access</h2><p>Sweat losses can change with pace, body size, acclimatization, clothing and weather. A useful training exercise is to compare body mass before and after representative long sessions while recording fluid intake, then use the pattern to improve planning.</p><p>The target is not necessarily to replace every millilitre while moving. The practical objective is to avoid a deficit or excess that meaningfully compromises comfort, safety or performance.</p></div></section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Endurance plan</p><h2>Four questions before race day</h2><div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--4"><article><h3>Where is fluid available?</h3><p>Map aid stations, carried bottles, refill points and the time between them.</p></article><article><h3>How much do you usually sweat?</h3><p>Use representative training sessions, not a single test in a different climate.</p></article><article><h3>Where does carbohydrate come from?</h3><p>Drinks, gels and food may all contribute. Do not assume a hydration product alone covers endurance fueling needs.</p></article><article><h3>What does your gut tolerate?</h3><p>Concentration, volume, timing and total carbohydrate load can affect gastrointestinal comfort.</p></article></div></section>

<section class="bhd-page__prose-grid"><article class="bhd-page__prose"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Best Hydrate in endurance</p><h2>Useful as hydration, not a claim to replace the rest of the plan</h2><p>Best Hydrate provides a prepared source of fluid, glucose and listed electrolytes when mixed according to the current label. In endurance settings, that can be one component of an athlete's intake strategy.</p><p>The product should not be framed as a complete endurance fuel. Longer events commonly require carbohydrate intake beyond what a lightly sweetened hydration formula is intended to provide, and sodium needs can vary widely between athletes.</p></article><aside class="bhd-page__callout"><span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Practice first</span><h3>Nothing new on race day.</h3><p>Test the exact drink concentration, bottle system, food pairing and timing in training before relying on it in competition.</p></aside></section>

